<h2>How Companies Adopt Ontologies — Cases · ROI · The Four-Step Build</h2>

<p>Where <a href="#!gdb-onto-concepts">the concepts part</a> covered "what an ontology is", this part covers what practitioners always ask next: are there real industry cases, does it pay (ROI), and how do you build it and who owns it.</p>

<h3>Where it is used — cases by industry</h3>
<p>Field practitioners agree on the conditions. Ontologies earn their keep where <strong>there are many systems, the relationships between entities are complex, and decisions span multiple departments</strong>. The cases follow those conditions.</p>
<ul>
    <li><strong>Finance</strong> — regulations change once or twice a week, and finding which internal systems each change touches is a real cost. Ontologies are used to trace quickly which systems an incoming unstructured regulation change affects, and for insurance claim adjudication and underwriting, where you must go beyond clause search and trace the grounds of a judgment.</li>
    <li><strong>Manufacturing · equipment</strong> — root-cause analysis of equipment failures. Vector search over maintenance history, work logs, manuals, and incident reports surfaces similar cases, but a question like "if we close this valve, which pumps, heat exchangers, and reactors does it reach?" is <strong>a causal chain</strong> that needs an ontology to answer precisely. Also semiconductor risk assessment (simulating in advance which hazard factors a design change touches) and quality history tracing (QMS) — tracking the cycle from customer complaint to factory floor to product change and back to customer satisfaction.</li>
    <li><strong>SCM</strong> — when inventory rises, was it a demand shift, a supplier's lead time, or a planner's strategic call? Customers, suppliers, production, purchasing, and sales are all entangled, so cause analysis needs the relationship structure.</li>
</ul>
<p>The end of this road is the <strong>digital twin</strong> — before you can simulate design changes digitally, you first move real-world entities and relationships into structure, and that structure is the ontology. Palantir, which commercialized this pattern, defines its ontology as exactly that: "the digital twin of an organization".</p>

<h3>Why it pays — ROI in three stages</h3>
<p>ROI evolves in three stages.</p>
<ul>
    <li><strong>Operating-cost savings</strong> — the intuitive early-stage effect. In one deployment, the time spent looking up and reviewing existing material at the design-verification stage (about 30% of the work) nearly disappeared (per the company's own account).</li>
    <li><strong>Automation and reuse</strong> — once structured, the data gets reused across other work and the scope of automation widens.</li>
    <li><strong>Changing revenue-linked work itself</strong> — yield calculation, production-system redesign: the work tied directly to revenue gets restructured.</li>
</ul>
<p>The consulting-side view adds an axis: <strong>decision speed and quality</strong>. A company that decides once every two days and one that can decide every ten minutes differ in opportunity cost — count the opportunities you used to miss, and the ROI range grows much larger than the cost savings. Traceable grounds sit on the same axis: when an LLM's answer cannot be backed up, practitioners use it only as reference and go find the grounds themselves; an ontology attaches <strong>a traceable trail</strong> — this answer came through these entities and these relationships.</p>

