<p>The steps themselves are ordinary: pick the work the problem lives in, select the data, structure it semantically. What deserves the stress is the last step — <strong>do not skimp on the verification cycle.</strong> The final blow against hallucination is evaluation, and each business must build its own machinery for checking, in cycles, whether the answers that come out are actually right: against an answer key when one exists, against the person who knows the answers best when one does not. The phrase "replace human limits with structure" points at this step.</p>
<p>Staffing runs on three tracks — an ontology engineer, an IT owner who knows the systems and schemas, and <strong>domain experts</strong>. The weight of that third track is the biggest difference from traditional IT projects: whether the meanings and relationships are right is ultimately a call only someone who knows the business can make. The working style has changed, though — instead of gathering the experts in workshops from day one, <strong>the tooling first drafts an ontology from the structured and unstructured data</strong>, and the experts verify right/wrong against something concrete. Reviewing a materialized draft is much faster.</p>

<h3>Governance — the organization breaks before the technology does</h3>
<p>Run the project and the bottleneck turns out to be governance, not technology. The same piece of equipment goes by different names in MES, ERP, and the maintenance system; data ownership is locked per department; KPIs split along departmental goals. The ontology is a frame that absorbs these mismatches technically, but <strong>which purpose (KPI) the ontology serves</strong> has to be settled by agreement first. Building an ontology resembles <strong>executives synthesizing departmental reports into a company-wide judgment</strong>. Which is why "everyone just give it a try" without top-level attention rarely works.</p>
<ul>
    <li><strong>Ownership</strong> — one or two departments (the AI org plus the business owner) carry it at first, but long term the whole workforce comes to share ownership and responsibility.</li>
    <li><strong>Tacit knowledge</strong> — veterans' know-how needs digitizing before it can be ontologized. These days even that step is faster: interview transcripts go into an LLM to draft process maps.</li>
    <li><strong>A living ontology</strong> — it is not built once and done. The structure must update as new data arrives, and above all <strong>executions and their after-the-fact evaluations must be stored</strong> — which decision ran in which conditions and whether it actually worked, failures included — so the next judgment gets better.</li>
</ul>

