<h3>How to start</h3>
<ul>
    <li><strong>Small and large in parallel</strong> — experience says start small in one work domain and expand stepwise, but large companies run a company-wide multi-year ontology plan alongside the small applications. Pick a first domain that can spread company-wide — where systems are badly disconnected, data is heavily entangled, or the decision structure is clear.</li>
    <li><strong>Time sense</strong> — the range quoted in the industry runs from three months on the short end up to a year and a half. Some run a PoC first, but companies that have already seen a peer's deployment increasingly go straight to the real project.</li>
    <li><strong>Work with specialists</strong> — as graph DBs and ontologies took off, in-house AI teams often self-study and try small builds, and the experience in the field is that these rarely work out. When choosing a vendor, look past the signboard at <strong>references and the design capability — how they would design an ontology for your business</strong>.</li>
    <li><strong>What you can prepare today</strong> — link the documents around your decisions (reports, meeting minutes) to their underlying data and to after-the-fact evaluations. One participant sums this up as <strong>"data debt"</strong>: like technical debt, the company that pays off the debt of context-less, value-only data first pulls ahead. The habit starts with storing <strong>temporal and spatial context along with the value</strong> — the same 100℃ means different things during normal operation and during maintenance.</li>
</ul>

<div class="note">
    <span class="nh">In one line</span>
    Cases cluster where relationships are complex and decisions are at stake; ROI starts as cost savings and grows into decision speed and quality; and the build succeeds or fails on the verification cycle and governance. An ontology is not a finished product — it is <strong>a structure that grows by feeding on execution and evaluation</strong>.
</div>

